  "worked_solution": "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,1).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=1.$ \n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(-4,2)$ and $(0,1),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2}{0-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=1$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4}x+1\n$$",
